 ## Overview
-Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy used in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to adjust the number and spatial distribution of Gaussian primitives during training [S1, S3][S11, unofficial]. It solves the problem that gradient descent alone cannot change the number of Gaussians or their coarse arrangement, leading to under-reconstructed regions or inefficient memory usage [S10, unofficial]. ADC periodically evaluates metrics such as gradient magnitude, opacity, and scale to trigger densification (cloning or splitting) or pruning [S3][S11, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static pruning methods (e.g., LightGaussian) by dynamically balancing addition and removal to maintain an optimal representation budget, rather than simply removing primitives [S1].
+Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy used in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to adjust the number and spatial distribution of Gaussian primitives during training [S1, S3][S11, unofficial]. It solves the problem that gradient descent alone cannot change the number of Gaussians or their coarse arrangement, leading to under-reconstructed regions or inefficient memory usage [S18, unofficial][S11, unofficial]. ADC periodically evaluates metrics such as gradient magnitude, opacity, and scale to trigger densification (cloning or splitting) or pruning [S3][S11, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static pruning methods (e.g., LightGaussian) by dynamically balancing addition and removal to maintain an optimal representation budget, rather than simply removing primitives [S1].
 
